```java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class VulnerableExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
        System.out.print("Enter your name: ");
        String name = scanner.nextLine();
        System.out.println("Hello, " + name);
    }
}
```
In this vulnerable code snippet, the `nextLine()` method is used to read user input without any input validation or sanitization. An attacker can exploit this by entering a specially crafted input that contains format specifiers, leading to a format string vulnerability.

***

To fix this issue, we should use a safer approach to read user input, such as using the `java.util.Scanner` class with proper input validation and sanitization. Here's the fixed code snippet:

```java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class SecureExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
        System.out.print("Enter your name: ");
        String name = scanner.nextLine();

        // Input validation and sanitization
        if (name == null || name.trim().isEmpty()) {
            System.out.println("Invalid input. Please enter a valid name.");
            return;
        }

        // Safe to use the input now
        System.out.println("Hello, " + name);
    }
}
```
In this fixed code snippet, we added input validation and sanitization to ensure that the user input is not empty or null before using it in the format string. This helps prevent format string vulnerabilities.

